Poisson-nucleus experiment (does a variable nucleus count lift beta-beta?)
adopted params: n_nuclei = 4.7160 -> round = 5;  n_target = 120;  reps = 400
hard ceiling at N = 5 nuclei: 4.20% (= 5/119, only if every nucleus is a resinol)

=== fixed-integer vs Poisson at the fitted n_nuclei (mean-preserving) ===
anchor     ref bb | fix bb   fix N (mean) | poi bb   poi N (mean) P(>=ref)  poi 5-95% band
softwood    3.76% |  3.41%          5.00 |  3.32%          4.84      40%  [0.84, 5.88]
poplar      5.13% |  3.41%          5.00 |  3.23%          4.76       9%  [0.84, 5.88]
high-S      4.57% |  3.62%          5.00 |  3.43%          4.66      23%  [0.84, 6.72]

Q1 (mean): Poisson leaves the mean beta-beta on the same line as fixed N
           (E[N] = n_nuclei), so rounding was not what pinned it.
Q2 (band): P(>=ref) and the 5-95%% band show whether any molecule reaches
           the reference -- the hard line is now a band.

=== mean nucleus count required to hit each reference beta-beta (Poisson) ===
if these differ, one GLOBAL n_nuclei cannot serve all three anchors
anchor     ref bb implied lambda
softwood    3.76%           5.49
poplar      5.13%           7.49
high-S      4.57%           6.13

fitted global n_nuclei = 4.72. The spread of implied lambdas is the obstruction;
Poisson widens the band but cannot move a single global mean to three targets at once.
